    <h1 class="article-title">How to Brief a Business Intelligence Analyst: Scope, KPIs and Data Access Checklist</h1>
    <p class="article-dek">Brief a business intelligence analyst with a clear scope of work, KPI definitions, data access checklist, deliverables, and acceptance tests.</p>

    <div class="article-byline">
      <div class="article-avatar" style="background:#4C9C8B;">NK</div>
      <div><div class="article-author">Nathalie</div><div class="article-role">Consultant, Tycoon Clap</div></div>
      <div class="article-time">4 min read</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-hero-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 160" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="28" y="24" width="184" height="112" rx="14" fill="#FDEED0"/><rect x="80" y="54" width="80" height="56" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#2E3A55" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M92 96l14-16 12 8 20-24" stroke="#E9584C" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></div>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <article class="article-content" id="articleContent">
        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p>A BI project brief should describe the decisions the report supports, the people who use it, the KPIs they agree on, and the data the analyst can access. Define the first release, assign a business owner, and write acceptance tests before build work begins.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated November 2026</p>
        
        <h2 id="decision">Start with a decision and a named owner</h2>
<p>A usable business intelligence analyst scope of work starts with the conversation the dashboard should improve. For example, the sales director needs a Monday view of qualified opportunities by region to decide which deals need follow-up. Name the director as the decision owner and nominate a CRM administrator who can resolve source questions.</p><p>Capture the audience, reporting cadence, current workaround, and outcome you want to measure. A useful baseline might be three hours a week preparing reports and two teams disputing the same total in every review. For role boundaries, see our <a href="business-intelligence-analyst-vs-data-analyst.html">BI analyst vs data analyst guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="scope">Write BI project requirements for the first release</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Inputs.</strong> CRM opportunities and a finance-approved region mapping. Start with the previous 12 closed months and the current month.</li><li><strong>Outputs.</strong> One Power BI report with a leadership summary and a deal-detail page, plus a KPI dictionary and refresh instructions.</li><li><strong>Users and access.</strong> Sales leadership can see all regions. Regional managers see records for their assigned regions.</li><li><strong>Cadence.</strong> Data is available by 8 a.m. Eastern on business days. The report displays the last successful refresh timestamp.</li><li><strong>Future work.</strong> Forecast modeling and commission calculations go into the backlog.</li></ul><p>Record assumptions beside the scope. If opportunity stage history is missing, agree whether historical conversion will use a trusted export or move to a later release.</p>
<h2 id="kpis">Define business intelligence KPIs before designing charts</h2>
<p>For every KPI, document its formula, reporting grain, date field, filters, source of truth, owner, and an example reconciliation. Specify USD or another currency, fiscal periods, and the reporting time zone. Agree how the report handles blanks, late-arriving records, and reopened deals.</p><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th scope="col">KPI</th><th scope="col">Example definition</th><th scope="col">Acceptance check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Closed-won revenue</td><td>Sum USD opportunity amount once per closed-won opportunity by close date, excluding test records.</td><td>Match the approved CRM export for the prior closed month after agreed exclusions.</td></tr><tr><td>Win rate</td><td>Won opportunities divided by won plus lost opportunities in the same close period. Show a blank when the denominator is zero.</td><td>Confirm 15 wins and 10 losses equal 60%.</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified pipeline</td><td>Sum open opportunity amounts in approved qualified stages as of the latest snapshot.</td><td>Reconcile against the CRM view captured at the same timestamp.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="access">Data access checklist</h2>
<ol><li>List each system, owner, environment, tables or exports, approximate volume, and available history.</li><li>Provide a representative sample, a field dictionary, join keys, and a few known edge cases such as duplicate customers or canceled orders.</li><li>Arrange approved read-only access through named identities or approved service credentials. Keep passwords and tokens in the approved vault.</li><li>Confirm how the analyst can access private systems and how the published report will connect in production. Assign owners for gateways, credentials, and refresh alerts.</li><li>Agree how to mask sensitive fields, what users can export, and when to remove consultant access. Test access using a representative viewer account.</li></ol><p>Have the analyst confirm the data supports each KPI before the report build starts. Missing fields, imperfect joins, and access delays belong in the project plan with an owner and a resolution date.</p>
<h2 id="deliverables">What to expect from a BI analyst</h2>
<p>Specify the BI analyst deliverables in the statement of work. A typical handoff includes the published report, editable source or agreed project files, data model documentation, KPI definitions, refresh and access instructions, and a walkthrough for the internal owner. If business intelligence consulting includes maintenance, document who handles failures, minor updates, and larger scope changes.</p><p>A practical data analytics project plan has four review points. Approve the KPI definitions and sample data, review a prototype, reconcile the production figures and verify permissions, then hand off to the owner. For formal sign-off, record the test period, expected totals and tolerance, representative viewer accounts, refresh schedule, defects, and approver.</p>
<h2 id="brief">Copyable BI project checklist</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Decision and owner</strong> — [Decision, accountable owner, when it is made]</li><li><strong>Users and first release</strong> — [Audience, filters, report pages, exclusions]</li><li><strong>KPIs</strong> — [Formulas, grain, dates, source of truth, owner]</li><li><strong>Data and access</strong> — [Systems, sample exports, join keys, approvals, privacy rules]</li><li><strong>Delivery and sign-off</strong> — [Review dates, reconciliation tests, approver, handoff owner]</li><li><strong>Success after launch</strong> — [Reporting time saved, usage, or operational outcome and review date]</li></ul>
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
        <details open><summary>What should a business intelligence analyst get on day one?</summary><p class="faq-a">Give the analyst a business objective, a decision owner, a list of users, existing reports, draft KPI definitions, and representative data samples or an approved access path.</p></details>
<details><summary>Who approves KPI definitions?</summary><p class="faq-a">Assign one accountable business owner for each KPI. Use the finance owner for financial measures and the relevant operational owner for sales, service, or inventory measures.</p></details>
<details><summary>What counts as a completed BI project?</summary><p class="faq-a">The agreed report and supporting files are delivered, KPI totals reconcile, permissions and refresh pass testing, and the internal owner can operate the solution.</p></details>
        </div>
